# Testing Gemma 4 (E2B-it)

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    # Gemma 4 is not in a Hex release yet, so point this at a Bumblebee checkout that
    # has it. Once released this becomes {:bumblebee, "~> 0.8"}.
    {:bumblebee, path: "/path/to/bumblebee"},
    {:exla, "~> 0.12"},
    {:kino, "~> 0.14"}
  ],
  config: [
    nx: [default_backend: EXLA.Backend]
  ]
)
```

## What this notebook checks

Five checks, cheapest first, so a failure tells you where the problem is:

| # | Check | Expected |
| - | ----- | -------- |
| 1 | One forward pass | `" Paris"` |
| 2 | Greedy chat generation | `"The capital of France is Paris."` |
| 3 | System prompt, multi-turn, arithmetic | `"Tokyo"`, `"Your name is Ada."`, `"391"` |
| 4 | Thinking mode | a `<|channel>thought` block |
| 5 | Sampling with Google's recommended params | fluent prose |

**Timings on a 32 GB M-series CPU.** Loading the model takes about 10 s. Check 1 runs in
roughly a minute. The first `Nx.Serving.run` on a serving has to compile the XLA graph,
which takes **10–15 min** at `sequence_length: 128` — it is not hung, and it only happens
once per serving. Runs after that are fast. Check 1 uses `Axon.predict` and skips that wait
entirely, so run it first.

## Setup

```elixir
alias Bumblebee.Text.Gemma4Chat

repo = {:hf, "google/gemma-4-E2B-it"}
```

If you already have the checkpoint on disk, point at the directory instead — it needs
`config.json`, `tokenizer.json` and `model.safetensors`:

```elixir
# repo = {:local, "/path/to/gemma-4-E2B-it"}
```

```elixir
{:ok, tokenizer} = Bumblebee.load_tokenizer(repo)
{:ok, generation_config} = Bumblebee.load_generation_config(repo)

generation_config
```

Load in **bf16**. At the f32 default the tied `lm_head` plus the per-layer-input embedding
table come to roughly 13 GB of params, which does not fit comfortably in 32 GB.

```elixir
{:ok, model_info} = Bumblebee.load_model(repo, type: :bf16)

model_info.spec
```

Sanity-check the spec before going further. `num_blocks` should be `35`,
`num_kv_shared_layers` `20`, `attention_window_size` `512`, and `layer_types` should have
`:full_attention` at indices 4, 9, 14, 19, 24, 29 and 34.

```elixir
spec = model_info.spec

%{
  num_blocks: spec.num_blocks,
  num_kv_shared_layers: spec.num_kv_shared_layers,
  attention_window_size: spec.attention_window_size,
  global_layers:
    spec.layer_types
    |> Enum.with_index()
    |> Enum.filter(&match?({:full_attention, _}, &1))
    |> Enum.map(&elem(&1, 1))
}
```

## Check 1 — a single forward pass

Gemma 4's `tokenizer.json` has an empty post-processor, so it adds **no `<bos>`**, and the
model degenerates badly without one. The `<bos>` has to be in the prompt string itself.
(Upstream Transformers behaves the same way — this is not a Bumblebee quirk.)

```elixir
inputs = Bumblebee.apply_tokenizer(tokenizer, "<bos>The capital of France is")

outputs = Axon.predict(model_info.model, model_info.params, inputs, compiler: EXLA)

next_id =
  outputs.logits
  |> Nx.slice_along_axis(Nx.axis_size(outputs.logits, 1) - 1, 1, axis: 1)
  |> Nx.squeeze(axes: [1])
  |> Nx.argmax(axis: -1)
  |> Nx.to_flat_list()
  |> hd()

Bumblebee.Tokenizer.decode(tokenizer, [next_id])
```

Expected `" Paris"`. If you get `" France"` instead, the `<bos>` went missing — that single
token is the difference between coherent output and a repetition loop.

## Check 2 — greedy generation

`strategy: %{type: :greedy_search}` — note the `_search` suffix; plain `:greedy` raises.
Greedy is used here because it is deterministic and therefore reproducible.

`sequence_length: 128` keeps the first-run XLA compile bearable. Raise it only if you need
longer prompts; compile time grows with it.

```elixir
greedy_config =
  Bumblebee.configure(generation_config,
    max_new_tokens: 64,
    strategy: %{type: :greedy_search}
  )

serving =
  Bumblebee.Text.generation(model_info, tokenizer, greedy_config,
    compile: [batch_size: 1, sequence_length: 128],
    defn_options: [compiler: EXLA]
  )

:ready
```

`Gemma4Chat.format/2` renders the `<bos>` and the `<|turn>role\n...<turn|>\n` frame exactly
as the checkpoint's `chat_template.jinja` does.

```elixir
prompt =
  Gemma4Chat.format([
    %{role: :user, content: "What is the capital of France? Answer in one sentence."}
  ])

IO.puts(prompt)

Nx.Serving.run(serving, prompt)
```

Expected `"The capital of France is Paris."` **This cell is the slow one** — it triggers the
XLA compile.

## Check 3 — system prompt, multi-turn, arithmetic

These three exercise instruction-following, KV-cache correctness across turns, and actual
reasoning. All reuse the compiled serving, so they are quick.

```elixir
Gemma4Chat.format([
  %{role: :system, content: "You are terse. Answer in exactly one word."},
  %{role: :user, content: "Capital of Japan?"}
])
|> then(&Nx.Serving.run(serving, &1))
```

Expected `"Tokyo"` — a single word, meaning the system turn was honoured.

```elixir
Gemma4Chat.format([
  %{role: :user, content: "My name is Ada."},
  %{role: :assistant, content: "Nice to meet you, Ada."},
  %{role: :user, content: "What is my name?"}
])
|> then(&Nx.Serving.run(serving, &1))
```

Expected `"Your name is Ada."` — recall across turns, so the assistant turn was rendered
with the `model` role and the cache is sound.

```elixir
Gemma4Chat.format([
  %{role: :user, content: "What is 17 * 23? Reply with just the number."}
])
|> then(&Nx.Serving.run(serving, &1))
```

Expected `"391"`.

## Check 4 — thinking mode

`enable_thinking: true` emits the `<|think|>` control token at the top of the system turn.
The model then produces a `<|channel>thought\n...<channel|>` block before its answer, so
give it more room.

```elixir
thinking_serving =
  Bumblebee.Text.generation(
    model_info,
    tokenizer,
    Bumblebee.configure(greedy_config, max_new_tokens: 256),
    compile: [batch_size: 1, sequence_length: 128],
    defn_options: [compiler: EXLA]
  )

Gemma4Chat.format([%{role: :user, content: "Is 91 prime?"}], enable_thinking: true)
|> then(&Nx.Serving.run(thinking_serving, &1))
```

91 = 7 x 13, so a correct answer is "no". Watch for the reasoning block in the output.

## Check 5 — sampling

Google's recommended settings for Gemma 4 are `temperature: 1.0`, `top_p: 0.95`,
`top_k: 64`. The shipped `generation_config.json` already carries them, so this is what you
get by default if you do not override the strategy. Output varies between runs.

```elixir
sampling_serving =
  Bumblebee.Text.generation(
    model_info,
    tokenizer,
    Bumblebee.configure(generation_config,
      max_new_tokens: 128,
      strategy: %{type: :multinomial_sampling, top_p: 0.95, top_k: 64},
      temperature: 1.0
    ),
    compile: [batch_size: 1, sequence_length: 128],
    defn_options: [compiler: EXLA]
  )

Gemma4Chat.format([%{role: :user, content: "Write one sentence about bees."}])
|> then(&Nx.Serving.run(sampling_serving, &1))
```

## Interactive chat

```elixir
message_input = Kino.Input.textarea("Message", default: "Explain RoPE in two sentences.")
```

```elixir
Gemma4Chat.format([%{role: :user, content: Kino.Input.read(message_input)}])
|> then(&Nx.Serving.run(serving, &1))
```

Multi-turn: append the model's reply as an `:assistant` message and re-render the whole
list. Per Google's guidance, drop any thinking blocks from previous turns — only the final
answer should go back in.

```elixir
history = [
  %{role: :user, content: "What is Elixir?"},
  %{role: :assistant, content: "A functional language on the Erlang VM."},
  %{role: :user, content: "What runs it?"}
]

Gemma4Chat.format(history) |> then(&Nx.Serving.run(serving, &1))
```

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause |
| ------- | ----- |
| `" France"` in check 1, or a `" France is France is"` loop | `<bos>` missing — use `Gemma4Chat.format/2` or prepend it yourself |
| `expected strategy type to be either :greedy_search or :contrastive_search` | you wrote `:greedy` instead of `:greedy_search` |
| A cell appears hung for ~10 min | first-run XLA compile; expected, and only once per serving |
| Out of memory / swapping | you dropped `type: :bf16`; f32 needs about 13 GB of params |
| `no matching mapping found for layer ...` | the local dir is missing `config.json`, or is not a Gemma 4 checkpoint |
| `mixture-of-experts checkpoints are not supported yet` | you pointed at the 26B A4B; only the dense sizes work |
| `cmake not found` | only affects `mix run` in the checkout (torchx). Livebook is unaffected — torchx is a dev/test-only dep |

Prompts longer than `sequence_length` are truncated silently, so raise it if you feed long
context — at the cost of a longer compile.
